--TEST--
Core\IO::writeStream refuses an ungranted path naming `fs.write`, in every spelling of its options bag and with an empty source as readily as a full one — so a member that reached its stream before it reached the door would answer something else on the last of them — `rule:core-classes/io-write-stream`, `rule:security/capability-question-is-grant-and-scope` and `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The claim is an ordering, and counting is what turns it into one. Four of
// these calls differ only in what the trailing shape says, so a member that
// read an option before asking would refuse for a different reason on one of
// them; the fifth hands over a source with nothing in it, which is the call a
// member that drew from its stream first would return from happily, having
// written nothing and asked nobody.
//
// Nothing is granted here, so the destination is never created either way --
// what is being pinned is the message, which is the operator's half: the
// capability's own name in the spelling `nvs.toml` grants it under.
//
// `Here::path` returns a literal resolved from this file's folder, so the path
// held in a variable is absolute when it reaches the door.
class Here {
    public static function path(#[Core\Path] string $path): string {
        return $path;
    }
}
array<bytes> $chunks = ["one " as bytes, "two" as bytes];
array<bytes> $nothing = [];
string $path = Here::path("stream.bin");
string $needs = "Core\\IO::writeStream needs the capability `fs.write` for " . $path . ", which is not granted\nhelp: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.fs]`";

int $calls = 0;
int $refused = 0;
int $namingFsWrite = 0;

try {
    $calls = $calls + 1;
    Core\IO::writeStream($path, $chunks);
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    if ($denied->message == $needs) {
        $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
    }
}

try {
    $calls = $calls + 1;
    Core\IO::writeStream($path, $chunks, {max: 4});
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    if ($denied->message == $needs) {
        $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
    }
}

try {
    $calls = $calls + 1;
    Core\IO::writeStream($path, $chunks, {overwrite: true});
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    if ($denied->message == $needs) {
        $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
    }
}

try {
    $calls = $calls + 1;
    Core\IO::writeStream($path, $chunks, {max: 4, overwrite: true});
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    if ($denied->message == $needs) {
        $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
    }
}

// The ordering half: an empty stream is still a write, and the door is asked
// about it before anything asks the source how long it is.
try {
    $calls = $calls + 1;
    Core\IO::writeStream($path, $nothing);
} catch (RuntimeError $denied) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
    if ($denied->message == $needs) {
        $namingFsWrite = $namingFsWrite + 1;
    }
}

echo "calls: ", $calls as string, "\n";
echo "refused: ", $refused as string, ", naming fs.write: ", $namingFsWrite as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
calls: 5
refused: 5, naming fs.write: 5
